<docs-article
  title="Working with AI"
  description="The marko-ui CLI feeds structured, machine-checked context to your coding agent to guide component discovery, installation, and Marko 6 authoring."
  href="/docs/working-with-ai"
  toc=TOC
>
  <h2 id="overview">Overview</h2>

  <p>
    Without marko-ui-specific context, a coding model's defaults are React: it invents
    <code>useState</code>, imports from <code>react</code>, and proposes props that don't
    exist on Marko components. Marko 6 is a different paradigm — resumable, tag-based,
    with a state model built around serialization — and off-the-shelf guidance for it is
    scarce.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <code>marko-ui agents sync</code> command solves this at the source: it writes
    a curated skill and an <code>AGENTS.md</code> section that teach your agent the CLI
    workflow, the Marko 6 authoring landmines, and which components are actually installed
    in your project. The CLI provides structured, machine-checked context through its manifest
    and a freshness check for generated docs. The skill contains curated guidance;
    run sync again when your installed components or CLI version change. See <a href="/docs/cli">CLI</a> for the full command reference.
  </p>

  <h2 id="quick-start">Quick Start</h2>

  <p>
    Paste this prompt into your AI agent. It handles both new and existing projects:
  </p>

  <code-block language="text" code=QUICK_START_PROMPT/>

  <p>
    The agent will run one of two commands depending on your project's state:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>bunx marko-ui init --agents</code> — first-time setup: initialises
      <code>components.json</code>, installs the base theme, and writes the agent docs
      in one step.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>marko-ui agents sync</code> — refresh the generated docs after adding or
      removing components.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="what-gets-generated">What Gets Generated</h2>

  <p>
    <code>agents sync</code> writes two outputs to your project root:
  </p>

  <h3>The marko-ui Claude skill</h3>

  <p>
    <code>.claude/skills/marko-ui/SKILL.md</code> — a structured skill file
    (Claude-specific path; other agents read <code>AGENTS.md</code> instead) that
    teaches the search → show → add → doctor workflow. Use the manifest
    for the live command sequence, including component documentation:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <for|step| of=CLI_MANIFEST.agentWorkflow>
      <li><code>${step}</code></li>
    </for>
  </ol>

  <p>
    The skill also includes a 9-item Marko 6 authoring-landmines catalog — common
    mistakes agents make when they apply React or Vue mental models to Marko — and a
    list of the project's actually-installed component names. The AGENTS.md
    index also includes registry descriptions when available.
  </p>

  <h3>AGENTS.md</h3>

  <p>
    A marker-delimited section is inserted into (or appended to) <code>AGENTS.md</code>
    in your project root. Everything outside the markers is preserved, so your own
    agent instructions and notes survive each sync unchanged.
  </p>

  <h3>CI gate</h3>

  <p>
    <code>marko-ui agents sync --check</code> exits 3 when the generated docs are stale
    relative to the installed components. Wire it into your CI pipeline to catch agents
    working from outdated context.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cli-contract">CLI Contract for Agents</h2>

  <p>
    <code>marko-ui manifest</code> prints a self-describing JSON document of every
    command, argument, and flag introspected from the live program, alongside
    the exit-code and error-code contracts. An agent can call it once to learn the full
    surface in a single step. The <a href="/docs/cli">CLI page</a> is generated from
    this same manifest.
  </p>

  <h3>Exit codes</h3>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Code</th>
        <th>Meaning</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <for|code| of=Object.keys(CLI_MANIFEST.exitCodes)>
        <tr>
          <td><code>${code}</code></td>
          <td>${CLI_MANIFEST.exitCodes[code]}</td>
        </tr>
      </for>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>JSON output envelopes</h3>

  <p>
    <code>docs --list --json</code>, <code>doctor --json</code>, and
    <code>registry list --json</code> return typed success envelopes with
    <code>$type</code>, <code>version</code>, <code>ok</code>, and
    <code>data</code>. For example, an empty documentation list looks like:
  </p>

  <code-block language="json" code=JSON_ENVELOPE/>

  <p>
    <code>info --json</code> (alias of <code>status --json</code>) and
    <code>search --json</code> also provide structured output, but currently return
    plain result objects without this envelope. <code>docs &lt;component&gt;</code>
    returns markdown; its JSON option applies to listing components.
  </p>

  <h3>Non-interactive flags</h3>

  <p>
    Use <code>-y / --yes</code> to skip confirmation prompts and
    <code>--silent</code> to mute progress output where supported.
    <code>add --dry-run</code> previews file changes; <code>init --force</code>
    overwrites existing configuration when that is intended. These are command-specific
    options: consult <code>marko-ui manifest</code> or the
    <a href="/docs/cli#commands">generated command reference</a> before using them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="skills">Skills</h2>

  <p>
    Three additional agent skills are available through the
    <a href="https://github.com/svallory/marko-ui" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer external">marko-ui skills pack</a>:
  </p>

  <code-block language="bash" code="npx skills add svallory/marko-ui"/>

  <h3>port-to-marko</h3>

  <p>
    A complete workflow for porting any React component library to Marko: inventory
    upstream components, port them with the SSR-safe Zag.js pattern, run mechanical
    parity verification (type check, hydration invariant, visual diff against the
    upstream), and publish through the official contribution or community registry path. See
    <a href="/docs/contributing-a-library#port-with-an-agent">Contributing a Library</a> for the full
    porting flow and the wanted-library list.
  </p>

  <h3>marko6</h3>

  <p>
    A Marko 6 language skill containing a compile-verified error catalog and
    language-level rules for reactive state, serialization constraints, and
    attr-tag patterns.
  </p>

  <h3>marko-run</h3>

  <p>
    Routing, request handlers, middleware, and deployment targets for
    <code>@marko/run</code>. Covers the adapter model, the route file conventions,
    and Node, static, and Netlify deployment adapters.
  </p>

  <h2 id="markdown-endpoints">Markdown Endpoints</h2>

  <p>
    Every component page is available as markdown at
    <code>/docs/components/&lt;name&gt;.md</code>. The CLI surfaces this directly:
  </p>

  <code-block language="bash" code="marko-ui docs button   # prints button's docs as markdown"/>

  <p>
    In an agent session, <code>marko-ui docs &lt;component&gt;</code> pulls the
    documentation straight into the agent's context — usage, props, keyboard
    contracts, and examples. The CLI fetches the markdown endpoint for the agent.
  </p>

  <h2 id="check-your-setup">Check Your Setup</h2>

  <p>
    Once you have run <code>agents sync</code>, paste this prompt to your agent to
    verify that the context landed correctly:
  </p>

  <code-block language="text" code=VERIFY_PROMPT/>

  <p>
    A well-configured agent should answer all three using the generated files and the documentation they link to without performing a web search. If it can't, it should
    run <code>marko-ui agents sync</code> first and re-read the files.
  </p>
</docs-article>
